### What is MapStruct?

MapStruct is a code-generation library for Java. You write an interface annotated with `@Mapper`, declaring methods that take an entity and return a DTO (or vice versa). During compilation, an annotation processor reads the interface and generates a concrete implementation class with plain getter-and-setter assignments.

```java
package com.bank.mapper;

import com.bank.dto.AccountResponse;
import com.bank.model.Account;
import org.mapstruct.Mapper;
import org.mapstruct.Mapping;

@Mapper(componentModel = "spring")
public interface AccountMapper {

    @Mapping(source = "id", target = "accountId")
    AccountResponse toResponse(Account entity);

    @Mapping(target = "availableBalance",
             expression = "java(entity.getBalance().subtract(entity.getPendingHolds()))")
    AccountDetailResponse toDetail(Account entity);
}
```

Because `componentModel` is `"spring"`, the generated mapper is a Spring bean that you inject into the service layer.

### How does MapStruct compare to manual mapping?

Manual mapping means writing copy code by hand for every field. It is accurate and explicit, but repetitive and fragile: when a developer adds a new field to both the entity and the DTO but forgets the mapper, the field silently returns `null` in responses.

```java
// Manual mapping – explicit but error-prone:
public AccountResponse toResponse(Account entity) {
    return new AccountResponse(
            entity.getId(),
            entity.getHolderName(),
            entity.getBalance()
            // Forgot the new "branchCode" field → silently null
    );
}
```

With MapStruct, the annotation processor matches fields by name automatically and warns about unmapped properties at compile time.

```java
// MapStruct – compiler warns if a DTO field has no matching entity field:
// Warning: Unmapped target property: "branchCode"
@Mapper(componentModel = "spring")
public interface AccountMapper {
    AccountResponse toResponse(Account entity);
}
```

### How does MapStruct compare to ModelMapper? (comparison table)

| Feature | MapStruct | ModelMapper |
|---|---|---|
| When mapping runs | Compile time | Runtime |
| How it maps | Generated assignments | Reflection + name matching |
| Performance | Direct field copies | Reflective calls (slower) |
| Error detection | Build fails or warns | Silent mismatch at runtime |
| Debugging | Step through generated code | Opaque reflective calls |
| Type safety | Full compile-time checks | None |

```java
// ModelMapper – runtime, reflective, may silently mismap:
import org.modelmapper.ModelMapper;

ModelMapper modelMapper = new ModelMapper();
AccountResponse resp = modelMapper.map(entity, AccountResponse.class);
// If entity renames "id" to "accountNumber", ModelMapper silently drops the value.
```

```java
// MapStruct – compile time, explicit, fails fast:
@Mapper(componentModel = "spring")
public interface AccountMapper {
    @Mapping(source = "accountNumber", target = "accountId")
    AccountResponse toResponse(Account entity);
    // If source field does not exist → compile error
}
```

### How do you handle differing field names?

The `@Mapping` annotation maps a source field to a differently named target field. If you forget the mapping, MapStruct warns about the unmapped target.

```java
@Mapper(componentModel = "spring")
public interface AccountMapper {
    @Mapping(source = "id", target = "accountId")          // different names
    @Mapping(source = "holderName", target = "name")       // different names
    @Mapping(target = "passwordHash", ignore = true)       // explicitly ignored
    AccountResponse toResponse(Account entity);
}
```

### How do you compute values during mapping?

For computed fields that do not exist on the entity, use the `expression` attribute to provide a Java expression.

```java
@Mapper(componentModel = "spring")
public interface AccountMapper {
    @Mapping(target = "availableBalance",
             expression = "java(entity.getBalance().subtract(entity.getPendingHolds()))")
    AccountDetailResponse toDetail(Account entity);
}
```

The generated code calls `entity.getBalance().subtract(entity.getPendingHolds())` as a plain Java expression, so it is type-checked and debuggable.

### What are common mistakes with MapStruct?

The most common mistake is ignoring the unmapped-target warning. MapStruct reports unmapped properties, and developers suppress the warning instead of handling it, which defeats the purpose.

```java
// Wrong – suppressing the warning hides potential leaks:
@Mapper(componentModel = "spring", unmappedTargetPolicy = ReportingPolicy.IGNORE)
public interface AccountMapper { ... }

// Correct – let warnings surface and handle each explicitly:
@Mapper(componentModel = "spring")
public interface AccountMapper {
    @Mapping(target = "internalRiskScore", ignore = true)  // explicit decision
    AccountResponse toResponse(Account entity);
}
```

Another mistake is placing the mapper invocation in the controller instead of the service layer. The mapper should be called inside the service, inside the transaction boundary, so that lazy-loaded fields are available if needed.

### What about performance at high throughput?

The generated code is plain field assignments with no reflection, so the performance is identical to hand-written code. On a hot path in a banking transaction service processing thousands of requests per second, the difference between MapStruct (nanoseconds of direct assignment) and ModelMapper (microseconds of reflection) adds up.

```java
// Generated code by MapStruct (visible in target/generated-sources):
public class AccountMapperImpl implements AccountMapper {
    @Override
    public AccountResponse toResponse(Account entity) {
        if (entity == null) return null;
        return new AccountResponse(
                entity.getId(),
                entity.getHolderName(),
                entity.getBalance()
        );
    }
}
// No reflection, no guessing – just assignments.
```

### Why is MapStruct a security asset?

Mapping is where security-sensitive decisions live. A response DTO should never carry a password hash, and the entity-to-DTO mapping is the exact point where that field is excluded. With MapStruct, the omission is visible in the interface and enforced by an `ignore`. When someone adds a new sensitive field to the entity, the unmapped-source warning forces a conscious decision about whether to expose it.

```java
// New sensitive field added to entity:
@Entity
public class Account {
    // ... existing fields ...
    private String ssn;  // newly added
}

// MapStruct warns: "Unmapped source property: ssn"
// Developer must decide: map it (expose) or ignore it (hide).
@Mapping(target = "ssn", ignore = true)  // explicit decision to hide
AccountResponse toResponse(Account entity);
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer invokes MapStruct in the service layer so the controller speaks only DTOs. They set `unmappedTargetPolicy = ReportingPolicy.WARN` to catch missing mappings without breaking the build, or `ERROR` for strict enforcement. They use `@AfterMapping` and `@BeforeMapping` hooks for complex post-processing, and they know that MapStruct supports Java records since version 1.5. In a banking microservices architecture, MapStruct is standard because it keeps the mapping explicit, fast, and reviewable in code review.
